      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Simple tenses in depth",
        "body": "Today the whole lesson is simple indicative tenses only. Do not study compounds yet. You must explain and form present, imperfect (past progressive / used to), preterite (simple past), future, and conditional as five clear time tools.\n\nEach tense answers a different question. Present: what happens or is habitual now? Imperfect: what was background or habit in the past? Preterite: what finished at a past point? Future: what has not happened yet? Conditional: what would be possible, polite, or hypothetical?\n\nDrill one regular -ar style verb in English patterns with sing / live / eat. Write five first-person forms and say them aloud: I sing / I used to sing (or was singing) / I sang / I will sing / I would sing. Mark the time meaning, not just the ending.\n\nKey contrast: imperfect vs preterite. «When I was a child, I read every night» (habit/background) versus «Yesterday I read the chapter» (finished). If the past action has a clear endpoint, prefer simple past. If you set a scene while something else happens, prefer imperfect/past progressive.\n\nClass practice: five minimal sentences with fixed frames — today / when I was a child / yesterday / tomorrow / if I could — using the same verb. Then change only the tense and explain in one sentence what nuance changed.\n\nError to fix: using imperfect when you mean a finished event («Yesterday I was going to the cinema» if you mean you went → «Yesterday I went»). Ask: did the action close at a concrete past moment?"
      }
    ],
